{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305209,"nonce":"GK7Av5ckXIiK1sAe1U/T6TrR0V8=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiIlw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/26","origin":"github"},"title":"No sparse-file awareness: unallocated regions of partial downloads are read in full","message":"Partially-downloaded files produced by qBittorrent, rTorrent and others are typically preallocated and **sparse** — the not-yet-downloaded regions are holes that occupy no blocks.\n\nThe tool reads them as ordinary data, so a 100 GB torrent that is 40% complete still reads 100 GB, 60 GB of which is kernel-generated zeros.\n\n`os.lseek()` with `SEEK_DATA` / `SEEK_HOLE` reports the allocated extents, letting those regions be skipped entirely. Because it reduces bytes read rather than speeding up processing, this is one of the few optimisations that can beat the hashing throughput floor.\n\nOne correctness detail makes this exact rather than approximate: a hole reads as zeros, and an all-zero piece has a real SHA-1. So precompute the digest of a zero-filled piece (and of the shorter final piece) and compare against it, instead of assuming a hole means absent data.\n\nNeeds a graceful fallback to \"assume fully allocated\" on `ENXIO` / `EINVAL` and on filesystems and platforms that do not support it.","files":null}]}